# i486 Doom indexed backend

The engine is doomgeneric commit `e257a09527062bfb986b6e5e0780839b6cbd5b71`
(GPL-2). `build.sh` fetches it into `/tmp` and does not commit it.
The host smoke uses Freedoom Phase 1, 0.13.0, `freedoom1.wad`
sha256 `7323bcc168c5a45ff10749b339960e98314740a734c30d4b9f3337001f9e703d`,
from the Freedoom 0.13.0 zip
`3f9b264f3e3ce503b4fb7f6bdcb1f419d93c7b546f4df3e874dd878db9688f59`.
Freedoom is BSD. The wad is not committed. A proprietary WAD is not used.
A shareware `doom1.wad` mirror returned 404, so no smaller IWAD is pinned.

`build.sh` produces `/tmp/wd24x-doom.s`
sha256 `e735b3fdb770780bc8ac4c97f7239cafb63103aae310f5b43d5c0e6bbd8a3164`
size 435064. i486 static-pie, no INTERP, no `cmov`. Built with
`-DCMAP256`. The engine zone minimum is 6 MiB (`DEFAULT_RAM` / `MIN_RAM`
in `i_system.c`). The wad is read through stdio into that zone. The
file itself stays in the RAM filesystem for the whole session.

## What the default binary does

`WD24X_DOOM_FB`, `WD24X_DOOM_PALETTE`, and `WD24X_DOOM_INPUT` unset:
no `/dev/fb0` open, no `FBIOPUTCMAP`, no tty open. It still composes a
private 640x480 index frame so the present can be checksummed.
`-nosound` is passed by the bench. Sound is not initialized. A fair
comparison with a Windows 98 run would use the same sound setting on
both sides. This bench is silent, so it does not match a run that
mixed audio. Detail is the engine default (high). Windows 98 "low
detail" is a different setting and is not selected here.

Render is 320x200 indices. `playpal0` is the engine's gamma-adjusted
PLAYPAL entry 0 at exit, decimal channels 0..255. The DAC form used
if a palette put is requested is `(channel >> 2) << 10`. For every
byte 0..255 that equals the fbcon `(byte << 8) | byte` value shifted
down by 10, which is what the colormap candidate stores. The host
test checks all 256 bytes.

`WD24X_DOOM_PRESENT=letterbox` (the default) writes each source pixel
as a 2x2 block at rows 40..439. The picture is 640x400 of index data
with 40 rows of index 0 above and below. Framebuffer pixels are square,
so that picture is 8:5, the raw buffer aspect. A CRT showed 320x200 as
4:3 by using tall pixels. Letterbox does not do that.

`WD24X_DOOM_PRESENT=fill` scales 2x horizontally and repeats source
rows in the pattern 2,3,2,2,3 (12 destination rows for each 5 source
rows). 320x200 then covers all of 640x480, which is the integer form
of that 4:3 stretch. Copies stop at each 64 KiB boundary and do not
write at or past `0x4b000`. The host test counts 4 forward window
changes and no span that crosses a window.

`WD24X_DOOM_FB=mmap` opens `/dev/fb0` only when the fixed screen is
640x480x8, line length 640, and the mapping covers `0x4b000` bytes.
The same present buffer is then stored through that mapping in window
sized pieces. Any other geometry prints `fb refused` and exits.
`WD24X_DOOM_PALETTE=put` sends 256 entries with `FBIOPUTCMAP` after the
mmap is open. Any other value, including `1`, does not. `put` without
`mmap` exits before the wad is used. This put is the candidate's
colormap contract. It has not been run on the 755C.

`WD24X_DOOM_INPUT=tty` saves the whole termios, clears `ICANON` and
`ECHO`, sets `VMIN` and `VTIME` to 0, and puts the saved struct back
on the normal exit and on `SIGINT` / `SIGTERM`. `SIGKILL` cannot run
that restore. Arrow keys are `ESC [ A/B/C/D`. A tty byte has no key-up,
so each recognized byte is one tap (down, then up) and a held key does
not keep running. `KDSETMODE` is not called. If the VT is stuck in
graphics mode, this read can time out. That is not treated as a reason
to change KD mode.

## Host pins, not the 755C

`qemu-i386-static -cpu 486`, Freedoom `demo1`, `-nosound`, letterbox,
two frames: index `0x00e988bb`, present `0x807cd9bd`, playpal0 `1 1 1`.
The full demo, twice: 7117 gametics, 7156 frames, index `0x3ac75f8d`,
present `0x506f4d2d`, playpal0 `1 32 1`. The engine also prints a
frames-per-second figure for that qemu process. The two runs did not
match each other (533 and 535 realtics). That figure is not a 755C
result and not a Windows 98 comparison. The present copy is included.
The bank-fault cost of the real mapping is not.

Native peak `VmHWM` on this host for the same demo was 8988 KiB. That
binary is not the i486 static one. The Freedoom file is 28795076 bytes
(28121 KiB). It remains in the RAM filesystem while the process runs,
and lump bytes are also copied into the zone, so adding the file size
to the RSS double-counts those copies. The file alone is larger than
MemAvailable figures recorded on the machine (19876, 19816, and 20916
KiB). Freedoom phase 1 does not fit in that free RAM. A conservative
sum of the file plus the measured RSS is 37109 KiB, above 36 MiB, and
that sum double-counts. Subtracting the 6 MiB zone from the RSS still
leaves about 30965 KiB, which is more than the recorded free RAM.
There is no 12 MiB guarantee.
